Make vm_ptr_t as wide as the guest's address space.

vm_ptr_t is a guest virtual address as seen by the host driver. It was
`unsigned long`, correct on a 32-bit host and on an LP64 Linux host, but the
coLinux driver on Win64 is LLP64 -- there `unsigned long` is four bytes, so
every guest address the monitor touches is truncated to 32 bits. On x86-64
that is all of them.

The Linux side is untouched: there vm_ptr_t is already `void *`, the right
width by construction.

--- a/include/linux/cooperative.h
+++ b/include/linux/cooperative.h
@@ -335,8 +335,18 @@
 /* Inside Linux, vm_ptr_t considered a valid pointer in its virtual address space */
 typedef void *vm_ptr_t;
 #  else
-/* But inside the host, the type is considered not to be a pointer in its own address space */
-typedef unsigned long vm_ptr_t;
+/*
+ * But inside the host, the type is considered not to be a pointer in its own
+ * address space.
+ *
+ * It must still be as wide as a guest pointer. `unsigned long` is not: Win64
+ * is LLP64, so it is four bytes there and silently truncates every 64-bit
+ * guest address -- and on x86-64 that is all of them, since the guest kernel
+ * lives at 0xffffffff80000000 and the passage page well above 4 GB. The width
+ * belongs to the guest, so state it outright rather than borrowing the host's
+ * word size.
+ */
+typedef unsigned long long vm_ptr_t;
 # endif
 
 typedef struct {
